  "band": "K-2",
  "focus": "Kindergarten owns the first layer of school-and-home talk: concrete nouns and actions children can touch, see, and act out, the position words that let them say where things are, the feeling words that let them say how they are, and the language of the classroom itself (listen, share, sort, match). Words are taught orally first, through gesture, picture, and story, and every unit gives children one or two words that unlock many others (between, same, different, problem). It differs from grade 1, which adds precision, sequence, and cause words and begins describing animals, habitats, and weather, and from grade 2, which handles multiple meanings, richer feelings, and early nonfiction language. Morphology is limited to the idea that two small words can make one new word.",
  "selection": "Words were chosen for how often a five-year-old needs them in school and home talk, how many other words they unlock (position, feeling, same/different, opposites), how often they live in beloved read-aloud language (brave, giant, castle, wish, surprise), and how well they carry over for multilingual learners (family, library, community, calm, magic, giant). Each unit holds one coherent idea: a routine, a concept family, a body of movement, a story situation. Language-of-instruction words (listen, sort, match, same, different, between) are mixed with everyday general words, and only three Tier 3 words appear (community, seed, soil), each in a unit with at most two. Deliberately left for grade 1: sequence words (first, next, then), cause words (because, so), weather and habitat vocabulary, and precise describing words (enormous, narrow). Deliberately left for grade 2: multiple-meaning words with several unrelated senses, richer feelings (disappointed, embarrassed), and nonfiction text-feature words.",
